Differentiating the above equation w.r.t, we get $\frac{\mathrm{dx}}{\mathrm{dt}}=2 \mathrm{a} \omega(\sin \omega \mathrm{t})(\cos \omega \mathrm{t})$
Again differentiating, we get

\begin{aligned}
& \frac{\mathrm{d}^2 \mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{dt}^2}=\mathrm{a}=2 \mathrm{a} \omega^2\left[\cos ^2 \omega \mathrm{t}-\sin ^2 \omega \mathrm{t}\right] \\
& =2 \mathrm{a} \omega^2 \cos 2 \omega \mathrm{t}
\end{aligned}

The given equation does not satisfy the condition for SHM [Eq. (i)] . Therefore, motion is not simple harmonic.

